<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<manifest>
  <!-- fabric: the gyre dependency set. Converted from .meta (mateodelnorte/meta),
       whose per-repo branches rode inside the URL string as a git clone branch flag. -->

  <!-- The checkout layout.

       RFD 0111 gives this stack six words that name a position in the code:
       transport layer, contract, interactor, entity, repository, and data
       source. "Service" names a deployment set, and ring, port, actor, and
       controller name runtime things. Six position words make six sides, so
       the workspace is one hexagon and the numbered directories are its sides.

         1-transport   the input that triggers an interactor
         2-contract    what a transport layer and an interactor compose against
         3-interactor  a process that performs actions on entities
         4-entities    the simulated things, and the actions on them
         5-repository  an interface that gets, creates, and changes entities
         6-datasource  an implementation of a repository

       The numbers carry the ring order, which the alphabet loses. A listing
       then prints the driving side first, the inside in the middle, and the
       driven side last, so the rule that all dependencies point inward reads
       as "every side points at 3 and 4".

       Every project sits on a side. There are no other directories: service/,
       lean/, engine/, authoring/, cassie/, vendor/ and doc/ all named how a
       thing was built or where it came from rather than what it does, which is
       the drift the six words exist to stop. A directory that names no position
       collects whatever nobody classified, and it grew to eight of them.

       A service is not a seventh side. RFD 0111 gives "service" to a deployment set rather
       than to a position, so it names a packing of sides instead of one of them, which is
       why `service-zone`'s README says a domain is a packing and holds no code, only the
       manifest that runs its members together. It still needs a directory, because a
       repository that is checked out needs one, so it takes `7-service/`: past the ring
       rather than on it, and sorting after the sides it packs. Naming it `service/`
       unnumbered is what RFD 0111 already refused, because an unnumbered directory is
       where whatever nobody classified collects.

       `0-` is not free for it. That number holds metadata about the fabric itself, which is
       a different kind of thing from either a side or a packing of them, and it now holds a
       project rather than a reservation. `0-infrastructure/logbook` is the record kept about
       this dependency set: the conventions, the ledger, the gates that enforce them, and the
       dated entries. It sits before the ring because everything on the ring is its subject.

       That project is what this repository stopped being. A repo manifest and the record
       kept about the work are two jobs, and one repository was doing both: `default.xml`
       beside a 540-line CLAUDE.md, 44 beancount books, a mix application and a USDA memory.
       The split leaves the manifest here, where repo reads it, and moves the rest into that
       project. It also deletes two special cases. The checks and the ledger both used to test
       for being at `.repo/manifests` and climb two directories to find the workspace; an
       ordinary project finds it by looking for `.repo`, which is what every other one does.

       The recomposition rule needs no change for any of this. `path` drops a leading digit
       and joins with the child, so `7-service/see-through` rebuilds `service-see-through`
       exactly as `1-transport/runpod` rebuilds `transport-runpod`.

       This repository is not among them. `repo init` already clones it to
       `.repo/manifests` and reads the manifest from there, so a `path="."`
       entry produced a second working copy: edits at the workspace root were
       invisible to repo, and `repo sync` reset the root over them. It cost an
       unpushed commit before it was removed. Work on this repository in
       `.repo/manifests`, which is the checkout repo actually reads.

       `path` decides the checkout directory and this repository chooses it.
       `name` is the name on GitHub and the two are independent, so a project
       sits on its side whether or not it was renamed. Recomposition asks them
       to rebuild each other because a path that stops matching its name is how
       a checkout drifts from what RFD 0111 decided, but it is a convention
       this repository imposes, not something repo requires, and where a name
       is not ours to change it is the convention that gives way. FIXED_NAMES
       in check_docs.py lists the five, each with its reason. -->

  <remote name="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" fetch="https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" />
  <remote name="v-sekai" fetch="https://github.com/V-Sekai" />
  <remote name="fire" fetch="https://github.com/fire" />
  <remote name="taskweft" fetch="https://github.com/taskweft" />
  <remote name="meshula" fetch="https://github.com/meshula" />
  <!-- Upstreams of libraries a project links. Nothing here is ours, and the entries below
       pin each one by commit rather than by branch: a library a build links is a value this
       manifest depends on, and the rule about stating them does not stop at our own code. -->
  <remote name="brunolevy" fetch="https://github.com/BrunoLevy" />
  <remote name="pmp-library" fetch="https://github.com/pmp-library" />

  <!-- No remote or revision here: every project states both, so a project that -->
  <!-- omits either fails at init instead of inheriting a default nobody chose. -->
  <default sync-j="16" />

  <!-- 1: the transport layers. Each terminates a client protocol and triggers an
       interactor. The two MCP servers are transport layers for an authoring client
       rather than a player one, which changes the protocol and nothing else. -->
  <project name="transport-asset" path="1-transport/asset" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="transport-fanout" path="1-transport/fanout" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <!-- Two implementations of one contract, and the language is what tells them apart. RFD 0123
       takes the unqualified names away from the C pair for that reason: a name that says which
       of two it is beats a name that implies the other is a variant. The C pair carries player
       traffic; the Python pair carries none and exists to disagree with it. -->
  <project name="transport-gateway-c" path="1-transport/gateway-c" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="transport-ingest-c" path="1-transport/ingest-c" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="transport-gateway-python" path="1-transport/gateway-python" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="transport-ingest-python" path="1-transport/ingest-python" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="transport-client" path="1-transport/client" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="transport-godot-mcp" path="1-transport/godot-mcp" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="transport-blender-mcp" path="1-transport/blender-mcp" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="transport-xr-grid" path="1-transport/xr-grid" remote="v-sekai" revision="main" />
  <!-- The odd one: a RunPod Serverless worker is a client of its own endpoint, so this
       terminates a queue rather than a socket. It carries no player traffic. -->
  <project name="transport-runpod" path="1-transport/runpod" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <!-- The same bus, reached from a terminal instead of a queue, so the interactor pair can be
       driven with no container, no registry and no cold start. -->
  <project name="transport-bus-cli" path="1-transport/bus-cli" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />

  <!-- 2: the contracts. Each is a model that both sides compose against, so the Lean
       workspaces that state a wire format, a lifecycle or a rule sit here rather than
       in a directory named after the language they happen to be written in. -->
  <project name="contract-wt" path="2-contract/wt" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="contract-entity-packet" path="2-contract/entity-packet" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="contract-connection-fsm" path="2-contract/connection-fsm" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="contract-http3-queue" path="2-contract/http3-queue" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="master" />
  <project name="contract-protocol" path="2-contract/protocol" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="contract-interest-mgmt" path="2-contract/interest-mgmt" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="contract-patch-verify" path="2-contract/patch-verify" remote="fire" revision="main" />
  <!-- The manuals publish GitHub Pages, and a Pages URL does not redirect on rename,
       so this name is a published address rather than only a repository name. -->
  <project name="multiplayer-fabric-manuals" path="2-contract/manuals" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />

  <!-- 3: the interactors. Each performs actions on entities. -->
  <project name="interactor-physics" path="3-interactor/physics" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="interactor-ward" path="3-interactor/ward" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="interactor-spatial-oracle" path="3-interactor/spatial-oracle" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="interactor-taskweft" path="3-interactor/taskweft" remote="taskweft" revision="main" />
  <project name="interactor-triangulation" path="3-interactor/triangulation" remote="fire" revision="legacy" />
  <!-- The libraries interactor-triangulation links, as projects rather than as submodules or
       subtrees. All three were submodules and all three were empty in every checkout here, so
       nothing compiled. A gitlink states its revision where no tool reads it and no person
       sees it in a diff, and leaves the directory empty until somebody asks git for it
       recursively. A subtree states the pin once, in a squash commit message, and copies the
       tree into a history that did not write it: 118 MiB for these two.

       A manifest entry states the remote and the revision in the file this project already
       reads on every sync. `revision` is a commit rather than a branch, because a library a
       build links is exactly the value the manifest rule is about.

       geogram carries submodules of its own, and vendoring one level does not reach them.
       The first attempt compiled until `geogram/third_party/OpenNL/nl.h` was not there.
       `sync-s` makes repo sync those as sub-projects, so the recursion belongs to repo. -->
  <project name="geogram" path="3-interactor/triangulation/libs/geogram" remote="brunolevy" revision="c40b4653e34519fffaeb82ca640bdd9c9778e324" sync-s="true" />
  <project name="pmp-library" path="3-interactor/triangulation/libs/pmp-library" remote="pmp-library" revision="f2fb04f4a4188a5c1ab137e83b96e62fa99c639f" sync-s="true" />
  <project name="interactor-sketch" path="3-interactor/sketch" remote="v-sekai" revision="main" />
  <!-- Two implementations of one interactor, and the language is what tells them apart, the
       same way RFD 0123 splits the gateway pair. Neither takes the unqualified name: a name
       that says which of two it is beats one that implies the other is a variant. They are
       held to the same gate by a check in the Python half that reads the C++ half's header. -->
  <project name="interactor-see-through-cpp" path="3-interactor/see-through-cpp" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="interactor-see-through-python" path="3-interactor/see-through-python" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <!-- The academic CASSIE project's own Unity application. Its name is the paper's. -->
  <project name="cassie" path="3-interactor/cassie" remote="v-sekai" revision="main" />

  <!-- 4: the entities, and the actions on them. The engine images and the assembly
       build the runtime the entities are simulated in, so they sit beside them. -->
  <project name="entities-gyre" path="4-entities/gyre" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-godot" path="4-entities/godot" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="gyre" />
  <project name="entities-lean-combat" path="4-entities/lean-combat" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-lean-loot" path="4-entities/lean-loot" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-lean-progression" path="4-entities/lean-progression" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-lean-rebac" path="4-entities/lean-rebac" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-lean-shared" path="4-entities/lean-shared" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-humanoid-rom" path="4-entities/humanoid-rom" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-images" path="4-entities/images" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-assembly" path="4-entities/assembly" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-parsec" path="4-entities/parsec" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="entities-model-explorer" path="4-entities/model-explorer" remote="v-sekai" revision="main" />
  <!-- Nick Porcino's reference skeleton. We have no admin on it, so we cannot rename it. -->
  <project name="LabRCSF" path="4-entities/LabRCSF" remote="meshula" revision="dev" />

  <!-- 5: the repositories. Each is an interface that gets, creates and changes entities.
       This side was empty until 2026-08-18, and it was not: `entity-store` had existed as an
       untracked directory in the workspace for a day, with no git repository behind it and
       nothing backing it up. An unlisted directory is the one state this manifest cannot see,
       which is the argument for listing a skeleton rather than waiting for it to be finished. -->
  <project name="repository-entity-store" path="5-repository/entity-store" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />

  <!-- 7: the services. Each packs sides into one deployable thing and holds no code of its
       own: the composition, the endpoint it runs on, why the membership is what it is, and
       the release ladder. `service-zone` and `service-behaviour` were held out of this list
       while their READMEs still said "edge plane", a compound RFD 0111 retired, because
       listing them turned `mix check words` red. Both now say "transport layer", which is the
       substitution the gate itself prescribes, so the words are no longer what decides it.

       `service-zone` is held out again, for a different reason and by a decision rather than
       by a gate. Its own README says "Not built": it states a packing whose quadlet units
       and Fly machine definition do not exist yet, so there is nothing behind it to sync. The
       work is on `service-see-through` and `service-behaviour`, and a listed project is one
       every sync clones, every `repo status` walks and every gate run reads — a per-sync cost
       paid across every desk for a repository nobody is editing. The remote keeps it and
       nothing is lost; it takes its entry back when the work reaches it. -->
  <project name="service-behaviour" path="7-service/behaviour" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="service-see-through" path="7-service/see-through" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />

  <!-- 0: the fabric's record of itself. Not a side, and not a packing of sides: every other
       project here is a subject of this one, which is why it sorts before all of them. It
       holds the conventions in CLAUDE.md, the ledger that books the hours, the gates that
       decide whether the documents are true, and the logbook. -->
  <!-- The agent configuration, checked out where Claude Code looks for it. `path=".claude"`
       is legal: repo's `_CheckLocalPath` bans only `.`, `..`, `.git` and `.repo*` as path
       components, so this gets an ordinary managed checkout with its gitdir under
       `.repo/projects/`. A single-segment path recomposes to itself, which is why the
       repository is named `.claude` and not `claude-config`.

       A repository rather than a `linkfile` into the logbook, which is what this nearly was,
       and the contrast is the reason the logbook below carries none. A symlink is invisible
       to every check here. `repo status` cannot see drift in it and nothing gates it, so one
       project's permission settings would have become every project's with no diff and no
       history behind the change. A checkout has a history; a link does not.

       Claude Code reads this path, which is what makes a project at the workspace root the
       right answer here and the wrong one for a CLAUDE.md. The root belongs to repo and to
       none of the fifty-four checkouts under it, so a document placed there is read by
       everything above a checkout while being no project's to answer for. -->
  <project name=".claude" path=".claude" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />

  <project name="infrastructure-logbook" path="0-infrastructure/logbook" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />

  <!-- 6: the data sources. Each implements a repository: durable state, a file format,
       or a reader that gets facts out of something that was not built to be queried. -->
  <project name="datasource-queen" path="6-datasource/queen" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="datasource-flow" path="6-datasource/flow" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main-fabric" />
  <project name="datasource-flow-project" path="6-datasource/flow-project" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="datasource-foundationdb" path="6-datasource/foundationdb" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="portability-consensus" />
  <project name="datasource-duckdb" path="6-datasource/duckdb" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
  <project name="datasource-flowref" path="6-datasource/flowref" remote="fire" revision="main" />
  <project name="datasource-flowref-decompiler" path="6-datasource/flowref-decompiler" remote="fire" revision="main" />
  <!-- Upstream's own name, mirrored: the CASSIE paper's recorded sketch dataset, and -->
  <!-- the Immersive Data Center Management flow tooling. -->
  <project name="cassie-data" path="6-datasource/cassie-data" remote="v-sekai" revision="master" />
  <project name="idtx-flow" path="6-datasource/idtx-flow" remote="v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric" revision="main" />
</manifest>
